{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 Calibri;}}\f0{\pard\sa240\fs32 \b Brabo Analytics ? Google Ads API Design Documentation\b0\par}{\pard\sa60\fs24 Internal marketing analytics and campaign management platform\par}{\pard\sa240\fs20 Company: Brabo Concursos / AprovaSim  |  Date: September 2026  |  Tool type: Internal use only\par}{\pard\sa120\fs28 \b 1. Overview\b0\par}{\pard\sa120\fs22 Brabo Analytics is an internal, in-house platform built and operated by our marketing team. Our company sells online preparatory courses for Brazilian public service exams and acquires students through paid media launches on Google Ads and Meta Ads, using our own ad accounts managed under our own Google Ads manager account (MCC). The platform is not sold, licensed, or offered to any third party. Its only users are our internal marketing employees.\par}{\pard\sa120\fs28 \b 2. Purpose of Google Ads API usage\b0\par}{\pard\fi-240\li480\sa60\fs22 \bullet  Automated reporting ETL: an hourly scheduled job pulls campaign-, ad group- and ad-level performance reports (cost, impressions, clicks, conversions, video metrics) for our own accounts and loads them into our PostgreSQL data warehouse (Supabase).\par}{\pard\fi-240\li480\sa60\fs22 \bullet  Full-funnel attribution: API report data is joined with our CRM (ActiveCampaign) and sales platforms (Hotmart, TMB) to compute CPA and ROAS per creative, using a shared ad naming convention (AD<number> prefix) propagated through UTM parameters.\par}{\pard\fi-240\li480\sa60\fs22 \bullet  Campaign management automation for our own accounts: budget pacing monitoring with alerts, pausing/enabling ads and campaigns during launch windows, bulk creative and keyword management, and bid strategy (target CPA) adjustments.\par}{\pard\sa120\fs28 \b 3. Architecture and data flow\b0\par}{\pard\sa120\fs22 The system consists of: (a) Python ETL scripts calling the Google Ads API REST endpoint (googleads.googleapis.com, GoogleAdsService.SearchStream / Search with GAQL queries); (b) a PostgreSQL warehouse hosted on Supabase storing daily aggregated metrics; (c) a FastAPI + Jinja2 web dashboard, protected by authenticated sessions with role-based access control, where our team views launch performance, attribution and debriefing reports. A scheduler triggers the ETL every hour with a rolling 3-day date window to capture conversion lag restatements.\par}{\pard\sa120\fs28 \b 4. Authentication and security\b0\par}{\pard\fi-240\li480\sa60\fs22 \bullet  OAuth 2.0 (authorization code flow) with a refresh token tied to our own manager account login.\par}{\pard\fi-240\li480\sa60\fs22 \bullet  Developer token issued to our MCC; all API calls target accounts under this MCC (login-customer-id header).\par}{\pard\fi-240\li480\sa60\fs22 \bullet  Credentials are stored in server-side environment variables, never exposed to end users or embedded in client-side code.\par}{\pard\fi-240\li480\sa60\fs22 \bullet  The dashboard has per-user authentication (HMAC-signed sessions) and product-scoped roles; no external or public access.\par}{\pard\sa120\fs28 \b 5. API services and typical queries\b0\par}{\pard\fi-240\li480\sa60\fs22 \bullet  GoogleAdsService.Search (GAQL) ? daily ad-level performance report: campaign.name, ad_group.name, ad_group_ad.ad.name, metrics.cost_micros, metrics.impressions, metrics.clicks, metrics.conversions, video metrics; segmented by segments.date.\par}{\pard\fi-240\li480\sa60\fs22 \bullet  GoogleAdsService.Search ? campaign status and budget queries for pacing alerts.\par}{\pard\fi-240\li480\sa60\fs22 \bullet  Mutate operations (campaigns, ad groups, ads, keywords) ? used during launch operations to create/duplicate ads, pause/enable entities and adjust target CPA on our own campaigns.\par}{\pard\sa120\fs28 \b 6. Volume estimate and why Standard access\b0\par}{\pard\sa120\fs22 Current usage fits within Basic limits; however we are scaling the number of managed accounts (separate accounts per product line and media buyer), adding near-real-time intraday budget monitoring, and increasing automation during launch windows. These bursts (hourly reporting across multiple accounts plus intraday mutate batches) will exceed the 15,000 operations/day cap on peak days. Standard access removes this ceiling while our per-request behavior remains unchanged and paginated.\par}{\pard\sa120\fs28 \b 7. Intended audience and distribution\b0\par}{\pard\sa120\fs22 Internal use only. The tool manages exclusively our own company's advertising accounts. It is not distributed, resold or exposed to third parties, has no external sign-up, and no third-party data is processed. As an internal tool, Required Minimum Functionality (RMF) requirements for third-party tools do not apply.\par}}